<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moulins</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Moulins, Allier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moulins is a town on the Allier river in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, capital of the Allier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. It was the seat of the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> from the 14th century, and their court made it a centre of the arts; its cathedral holds the triptych of the Master of Moulins, painted around 1500. After the treason of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-duke-of-bourbon">Constable of Bourbon</a> in 1523, the duchy was confiscated and joined to the French crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moulins.jpg" alt="Moulins" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alphanumeric · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moulins_-_Perspective3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
